@*
    ── משאבים (Student) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The student gets THE SAME Resources product as the mentor and the
    lecturer — the approved screen in ResourcesLibrary, not a student variant
    of it. Search, the stage strip, the category chips, the resource cards and
    the detail panel are the identical components; role changes what the user
    may DO and which question the strip answers, not what the screen looks
    like. The 387-line kc- stylesheet this page used to carry is gone: it was
    the same composition redrawn, and every one of its differences from the
    lecturer sheet was drift rather than a product decision.

    READ-ONLY, BY CONSTRUCTION
      • CanManage is false — no "ניהול משאבים" link and no route into the
        management UI (which is Admin/Staff-only regardless).
      • The only actions are the read ones the shared detail panel exposes:
        download, open in a new tab, play a video.
      • GetPublicAsync reads api/student/resources, [Authorize(Student)] —
        the only list endpoint a student may call. It returns the same corpus
        the other two roles read, so nothing is filtered away and nothing is
        widened.

    THE STRIP ANSWERS A DIFFERENT QUESTION HERE
    For staff it means "where is the cohort"; for a student it means "where am
    I". So the copy is theirs, and the source is their OWN project's current
    milestone — StudentDashboardModel.CurrentMilestone over
    api/projects/my-dashboard, the same single source of truth the dashboard
    stepper, the focus card and the tasks page read. A student cannot call
    api/dashboard at all (Admin/Staff/Mentor only), which is exactly why
    ResourcesLibrary takes a stage DELEGATE rather than a scope string.

    AND IT DOES NOT FALL BACK
    AllowStageFallback is false. The staff strip may show any milestoned
    resource when nothing matches, because there it is a shortcut into a
    library they own. Under "רלוונטי לשלב שלך" that would be a false claim of
    personal relevance, so when nothing is pinned to the student's milestone
    the strip simply does not render — the behaviour this screen already had.

    The strip also stays visible while searching (HideStageWhileSearching is
    false), which is what the final student design shipped with.
*@

@page "/learning"
@layout AppLayout
@attribute [Authorize(Roles = Roles.Student)]
@using AuthWithAdmin.Client.Pages.Dashboard

@inject IResourceFilesService ResourceFilesService
@inject IDashboardService     DashboardService

<ResourcesLibrary Title="משאבים"
                  Subtitle="תבניות, מדריכים וכלים — מה שצריך כדי להתקדם"
                  Loader="ResourceFilesService.GetPublicAsync"
                  FavoritesKey="motiva.student.resources.favorites"
                  CurrentStageLoader="LoadCurrentStageAsync"
                  StageTitle="רלוונטי לשלב שלך"
                  StageNote="מבוסס על שלב הפרויקט הנוכחי שלכם"
                  AllowStageFallback="false"
                  HideStageWhileSearching="false"
                  CanManage="false"
                  EmptyDescription="כשצוות הקורס יעלה מסמכים, מצגות או סרטונים, הם יופיעו כאן." />

@code {
    /// <summary>The student's real current milestone title, or null when they
    /// have no project, no milestones, or the dashboard call failed. Matched
    /// against a resource's MilestoneLabel by title, because that is the only
    /// join the two payloads share: MilestoneSummaryDto.Title and
    /// ResourceFileDto's MilestoneName are both MilestoneTemplates.Title.</summary>
    private async Task<string?> LoadCurrentStageAsync()
    {
        var dashboard = await DashboardService.GetDashboardAsync();
        if (dashboard is null || dashboard.Milestones.Count == 0) return null;

        var current = StudentDashboardModel.CurrentMilestone(dashboard.Milestones);
        return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(current?.Title) ? null : current!.Title;
    }
}
